{"notes":"VERIFIED 2026-11-25. The library fails CLOSED. The defect is harness-only, and consumers are not exposed.

## pkix-chain's real validator propagates every revocation error

pkix-chain/src/lib.rs, Verifier::run_revocation_checks:

    for target in crate::sans_io::revocation_targets(chain, self.anchors, validated) {
        match target {
            RevocationTarget::Issuer { cert, issuer } => {
                self.rev_checker.check_revocation(cert, issuer)?;
            }
            RevocationTarget::Anchor { cert, anchor } => {
                self.rev_checker.check_revocation_against_anchor(cert, anchor)?;
            }
        }
    }

A bare `?` in both arms. No match, no catch, no softening. Every Err propagates and fails the validation, including CrlUnsupportedCriticalExtension and every OutOfScope variant.

Confirmed there is no soft-fail mode anywhere in the real path: no soft_fail, revocation_soft, allow_undetermined or ignore_revocation knob exists in pkix-chain, pkix-path or pkix-revocation. verify_chain is hard-fail only.

pkix-revocation also has direct unit coverage asserting Err(CrlUnsupportedCriticalExtension) -- tests/review_crl.rs and tests/crl_rfc5280_gaps.rs -- so the library behaviour is pinned by tests independent of the diff harness.

## So the picture is now complete and the library is exonerated

  pkix-chain::verify_chain     every revocation Err fails the chain   HARD-FAIL, RFC-correct
  diff-harness oracle adapter  only Revoked fails; all Err ignored    SOFT-FAIL, the defect

Commit a407f615 correctly made a CRL with an unrecognized critical extension unusable per RFC 5280 sections 5.2 and 5.3. Real consumers get a hard failure, which is the intended and conformant outcome. Only the harness's deliberately-soft oracle turns that into a pass.

If anything the library is now STRICTER for consumers than before a407f615, not looser. The direction I originally alarmed about was backwards.

## One design observation, not a bug

Because verify_chain propagates every revocation error, a caller supplying a CRL that merely does not cover the certificate gets a hard failure via OutOfScope. The library documents that hard-fail callers should treat OutOfScope as a failure, and verify_chain is consistent with that -- but it means there is no supported way to ask for soft-fail revocation. That is worth knowing and may deserve its own bead if a consumer ever wants OCSP/CRL best-effort. It is not part of this issue.

## Remaining work, unchanged in substance

The harness oracle needs to distinguish "CRL applied, certificate not listed" from "no CRL could be applied at all", and fail closed for the latter, so it models a PKITS-conformant validator again.

## Acceptance

[x] Determined whether pkix-path's own Verifier fails open on an unusable CRL -- it does NOT; it propagates every error with `?`, there is no soft-fail mode, and the behaviour is pinned by pkix-revocation unit tests
[ ] Harness adapter distinguishes "no usable CRL" from "CRL applied, not revoked", and fails closed for the former
[ ] Re-run and confirm 4.15.4, 4.4.8, 4.4.9 return to failing and 4.15.7 stays fixed
[ ] Baseline regenerated afterwards, covering the pyca 50.x drift and the 4.15.7 fix, with the reason recorded"}
